    "title": "2026 Oscars see rare tie for Live Action Short Film award",
    "text": "by: Jocelyn Fiset Posted: Mar 15, 2026 / 05:36 PM PDT Updated: Mar 15, 2026 / 05:36 PM PDT In a rare moment for the Academy Awards, the Oscars saw two nominees tie for the Live Action Short Film award. “It’s a tie, I’m not joking. It’s actually a tie, so everyone calm down. We’re gonna get through this,” said Kumail Nanjiani, who was presenting the award. Sam A. Davis and Jack Piatt won the Oscar for “The Singers” and Alexandre Singh and Natalie Musteata won for “Two People Exchanging Saliva.” Nanjiani called the first winner, “The Singers,” and let them finish their speech before announcing the second winner, “Two People Exchanging Saliva.” Host Conan O’Brien marked the unusual moment with the joke, “I just want to say congratulations to both winners. You just ruined 22 million Oscar pools.” From left, David Breschel, Mike Yung, Sam A. Davis and Jack Piatt accept the award for live action short film for “The Singers” after tying in ...From left, David Breschel, Mike Yung, Sam A. Davis and Jack Piatt accept the award for live action short film for “The Singers” after tying in the category during the Oscars on Sunday, March 15, 2026, at the Dolby Theatre in Los Angeles. (AP Photo/Chris Pizzello)From left, David Breschel, Mike Yung, Sam A. Davis and Jack Piatt accept the award for live action short film for “The Singers” after tying in the category during the Oscars on Sunday, March 15, 2026, at the Dolby Theatre in Los Angeles. (AP Photo/Chris Pizzello) This was not the first time there has been a tie in Oscars history. The rare occurrence has only happened six other times. The 98th Academy Awards were held Sunday evening in Hollywood at the Dolby Theatre on Hollywood Boulevard.",
